scope: |
  SSC3-14 — `math`, planned in `v3/SPRINT.md` on 2026-08-13 and not started. (That file is claimed
  by `bench-b4-three-version-table`, so this claim carries the plan, as SSC3-14b/c/13c/13d did.)

  THE DATED MEASUREMENT WAS RE-TAKEN BEFORE CLAIMING, because a verdict is dated evidence. All four
  cases still refuse with `unknown name 'math'`: `arithmetic` (17:9), `case-classes` (14:30),
  `js-scala-fenced-block` (15:38), `sealed-traits` (20:27). v2 still has `f.sqrt`, `f.floor`,
  `f.ceil`, `f.round`, `f.trunc` and still has NO `f.pow`.

  THIS ONE SHOULD ACTUALLY MOVE N, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN HOPED — the lesson of the three
  N-neutral changes before it. I pasted a hand-written `object math` into a copy of each of the four
  cases and ran them: ALL FOUR run to completion, so `math` is the LAST blocker in each, not merely
  the first. Expect N 212 -> 216 if the output matches.

  THE OUTPUT IS THE HARD PART, not the arithmetic. `tests/conformance/expected/arithmetic.txt` wants
  `4`, `7`, `256` for `math.sqrt(16.0)`, `math.abs(-7)`, `math.pow(2.0, 8.0)` — a whole Double
  renders without `.0` here — so "it runs" is not the bar and a hand-rolled Newton sqrt is not
  acceptable.

  THE SPLIT IS DECIDED BY WHAT v2 HAS, exactly as `hostPrims` was, because a v3-only prim breaks I-3:
    sqrt, round, floor, ceil   -> v2 prims, reached through an `extern` in the prelude
    Pi, abs                    -> pure ScalaScript, no prim
    pow                        -> NO v2 prim; an INTEGER exponent is a loop and needs nothing

  MATCH v2 EXACTLY, NOT SCALA. `v2/src/Runtime.scala:1437` implements `f.round` as `math.rint` —
  banker's rounding returning a Double — NOT Scala's `math.round`, which is half-up and returns a
  Long. They agree on the corpus's values and disagree at `.5`, so v3's executor must use `rint` or
  the two lanes diverge on a case nobody has written yet. Same for the other three.

  BOTH LANES, and that is where the work is: the bridge passes a prim through to v2, which already
  has these, while `Exec` implements prims by name and has only the `io.*` family — so `Exec` gains
  four cases or `ssc3 run` refuses what `ssc3 run --bridge` computes. That is the exact shape of the
  divergence closed this morning in `v3-flatmap-nonlist-lane-divergence`, so it gets parity probes
  in the same commit rather than a promise.

  `pow` IS DELIBERATELY PARTIAL: an integer exponent is a multiply loop; a FRACTIONAL one needs
  `exp`/`log`, which neither lane has, and must REFUSE by name rather than silently return a wrong
  number. Measured: no corpus case uses a fractional exponent — `arithmetic` writes `pow(2.0, 8.0)`
  and `js-scala-fenced-block` writes `pow(x - o.x, 2)`, both whole.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 and CRASH 9, the four cases read INDIVIDUALLY against their
  checked-in expectations, and lane agreement probed for every new prim.
  MEASURED 2026-08-14: N 212 -> 216, DIFF 3 and CRASH 9 held, UNSUPPORTED 143 -> 139. All four
  cases MATCH their checked-in expectations byte for byte. The prediction made before the edit was
  +4 and the result is +4 — the first N movement in five changes, and the difference from the four
  N-neutral ones is that the yield was measured with a pasted `math` object before any code existed.

  WIDENED 2026-08-14 to `v3/BUGS.md`, BEFORE the edit, for TWO defects this work found:

  (1) `v3-extern-member-in-an-object-has-no-meaning`. I wired the four host hooks as members of
  `object math` and neither front carries `extern` into an object: v3's parser refuses it outright,
  uniml DROPS the keyword and leaves the member with the same `???` body it gives any bodyless one
  — an unpositioned run-time failure, which `corpus-report.sh` classifies CRASH. Worked around by
  declaring them at top level with a `__` prefix; the `Lower` change that would have resolved
  externs over object members is reverted rather than shipped dead.

  (2) `v3-math-pow-fractional-needs-a-v2-prim`. The owner asked for the fractional case to be fixed.
  Measured what a fix must match: the reference lane is libm-exact (`pow(2.0, 0.1)` is
  1.0717734625362931), so a ScalaScript series or the repeated-sqrt expansion — tempting because
  both lanes have `f.sqrt` — would differ in the last bits and make v3 disagree with the reference
  on every fractional pow. v3 cannot do it on one lane either: the bridge emits a prim and v2 has
  no `f.pow`, `f.exp` or `f.log`. The fix is ONE LINE in `v2/src/Runtime.scala` beside `f.sqrt`,
  and that file is held by `rust-toint-lane-parity`, so it is asked for in the room and filed, not
  taken. Second thing today blocked on that file — see the `v2-bytecode-map-ops` release note.
